Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Geng, C"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"This course will be held in the Conference room at the Department of Asian American Studies at 1208 W Nevada, Urbana."}]},{"id":"LLS 308","title":"Spanish in the United States","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"59989","code":"D","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"312 Davenport Hall","instructors":["Pantoja, A"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"64939","code":"G","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":["W","F"],"start":780,"end":830,"location":"","instructors":["Henshaw, F"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Students must meet the prerequisite requirements for this course (found in the Catalog Entry) or will be told to drop on the first day of classes. Non-registered students are not allowed to sit in or audit. Students registered in LLS308 are not required the prerequisite."},{"crn":"68663","code":"X","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":570,"end":650,"location":"G20 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Trybulec, E"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Students must meet the prerequisite requirements for this course (found in the Catalog Entry) or will be told to drop on the first day of classes. Non-registered students are not allowed to sit in or audit. Students registered in LLS308 are not required the prerequisite. Same as LLS 308."}]},{"id":"LLS 310","title":"Race and Cultural Diversity","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"33081","code":"D1","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["R"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"320 Mumford Hall","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"All students must register for the lecture and one discussion section."},{"crn":"45582","code":"D2","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["R"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"42A Education Building","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"All students must register for the lecture and one discussion section."},{"crn":"45587","code":"D3","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["R"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"313 Mumford Hall","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"45599","code":"D4","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["R"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"136 Davenport Hall","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"76833","code":"D5","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["R"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"243 Armory","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"33079","code":"L","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T"],"start":840,"end":950,"location":"2 Education Building","instructors":["Moton, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"All students must register for the lecture and one discussion section."}]},{"id":"LLS 316","title":"Latina/Latino Politics","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"60394","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"103 1207 W Oregon","instructors":["Rodriguez, R"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 355","title":"Race and Mixed Race","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"57159","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"103 1207 W Oregon","instructors":["Rodriguez, R"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 357","title":"Literatures of the Displaced","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"70257","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"103 1207 W Oregon","instructors":["de la Garza Valenzuela, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 360","title":"Contemporary US Latina/o Lit","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"36903","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":780,"end":860,"location":"325 David Kinley Hall","instructors":["de la Garza Valenzuela, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Topic: The course will focus on the most recent publications of canonical Chicano/Latino writers: Richard Rodriguez, Junot Diaz, Luis Alberto Urrea, among others. The students will focus on how these writers navigate contemporary discussions on immigration, identity, me-too movement, social constructions of masculinity, etc. Students will learn how a focused analysis of gender, ethnicity and class affects the construction of Latino identity formation."}]},{"id":"LLS 372","title":"Immigration, Law, and Rights","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"77067","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":780,"end":860,"location":"214 Ceramics Building","instructors":["Kang, S"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 390","title":"Independent Study","creditHours":"0 TO 3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"10539","code":"","type":"Independent Study","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Independent Study","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 396","title":"Adv Topics Latina/o Studies","creditHours":"2 OR 3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"51103","code":"AW","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Waner, A"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Meets with SPAN 395. Title: Unwritten Textualities: Indigenous Literatures and Beyond Course Description: This course will introduce students to various forms of Indigenous textualities and the connections between them. Often, Indigenous literatures are placed in binaries and in a western chronological timeline, from oral to written and from traditional to modern. Students will read poetry, oral stories, newspapers, zines, and textiles amongst other forms to expand understandings of Indigenous literatures. While themes will vary, they will include identity, history, and politics. Course will be taught in English and readings will be provided in English, Spanish, and various Indigenous languages.","title":"Unwritten Textualities"},{"crn":"51046","code":"EG","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":630,"end":710,"location":"133 1207 W Oregon","instructors":["Guzman, E"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Topic: \"Visualizing Borderlands\": Over the past two decades, more than seventy border walls have been constructed worldwide in the name of sovereignty and security. These physical barriers are accompanied by legal regimes that restrict mobility, intensify surveillance, and reinforce “us versus them” nationalisms. This course examines how borders are built, enforced, inhabited, and contested, with particular attention to Latin American and U.S. Latino contexts. We will analyze how borders are represented, visualized, maintained, and contested in media, art, and literature. We will investigate not only geopolitical borders, but also linguistic, racial, gendered, and embodied boundaries that shape Latinidad and diaspora. Drawing from scholarship, literature, film, photography, and sound, students will explore how artists, activists, and communities represent, navigate, and reimagine borderlands as sites of struggle, creativity, and belonging.","title":"Visualizing Borderlands"},{"crn":"52339","code":"EVS","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"B","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":900,"end":1070,"location":"230 Davenport Hall","instructors":["Velasquez Estrada, E"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"LLS 396: Empiring Central America: Migration and Belonging Why do Central Americans leave home and risk their lives to cross borders? This course examines the root causes of migration. Using history, culture, lived experience, political struggle, forms of resistance, diaspora, and transnational life as analytical frameworks, students will examine migration from Central America. The course explores how U.S. foreign policy, security regimes, and economic policies shape migration, deportation, resource extraction, gang violence, inequality, and gender violence in the region. We will consider the conditions that force youth, women, mestizos, campesinos, Indigenous peoples, and Afro-descendant communities to leave, and how migrants maintain transnational ties and create new forms of belonging across borders. Course materials include academic books, scholarly articles, memoirs, and film."}]},{"id":"LLS 412","title":"Social Work with Latine","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"48452","code":"B","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":["W"],"start":1080,"end":1170,"location":"","instructors":["Ramirez, X"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 435","title":"Commodifying Difference","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"60411","code":"G","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"327 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Peoples, G"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""},{"crn":"40507","code":"U","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"327 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Peoples, G"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 442","title":"Latina Literature","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"65282","code":"G","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":600,"end":680,"location":"13 Sch of Info Sciences Bldg","instructors":["Bonilla, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""},{"crn":"52962","code":"U","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":600,"end":680,"location":"13 Sch of Info Sciences Bldg","instructors":["Bonilla, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 449","title":"Issues in Latina/o Education","creditHours":"2 TO 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"78837","code":"G","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["W"],"start":990,"end":1160,"location":"382 Education Building","instructors":["Corona Valencia, G"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""},{"crn":"78839","code":"UG","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["W"],"start":990,"end":1160,"location":"382 Education Building","instructors":["Corona Valencia, G"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 460","title":"Critical Ethnic Studies","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"79010","code":"","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["W"],"start":840,"end":1010,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Rana, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 490","title":"Research and Writing Seminar","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"67411","code":"A","type":"Seminar","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Seminar","days":["T"],"start":900,"end":1040,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Martinez, A"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 495","title":"Senior Honors Thesis","creditHours":"2 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"54582","code":"","type":"Independent Study","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Independent Study","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 590","title":"Independent Study","creditHours":"1 TO 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"46522","code":"","type":"Independent Study","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Independent Study","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LLS 596","title":"Graduate Seminar in LLS","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"51274","code":"GRS","type":"Seminar","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Seminar","days":["R"],"start":660,"end":800,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Rosas, G"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Topic: Be it in Europe, the Americas, the United States, or elsewhere in the globe, there has been belligerent calls to tighten international borders, and better regulate, who can settle, who can migrate, who must leave, and who should be held. Detention, policing, and the surveillance of immigrants and refugees has augmented exponentially. Keeping the pressing presence of the present central, the course moves through theoretical shifts underscoring the frictions among questions of movement, borders, migrations, and refugee studies with respect to the debates on abolition, biopolitics, settler colonialism, and other currents. Meets with: AFRO 597, ANTH 515, GWS 590, SOC 596.","title":"Critical Border Studies"},{"crn":"47295","code":"NF","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M"],"start":840,"end":980,"location":"150 English Building","instructors":["Flores, N","Maroun, D"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"HRI Interseminars: Remembering/Reimagining HIV/AIDS in Contemporary Times: This interdisciplinary course explores HIV/AIDS as both a biomedical condition and a socially constructed phenomenon. Through frameworks that examine the relationship between individuals, society, medicine, and technology, students will analyze how HIV/AIDS has been represented, contested, and experienced across time and space. Drawing from the humanities and social sciences, the course investigates the epidemic’s intersections with race, class, gender, and sexuality, while engaging with activism, policy, and cultural production. Emphasis is placed on critical analysis of historical texts, ethnographies, and media, fostering civic imagination and insight into how health crises reveal broader questions of identity, inequity, and social change."}]}]